WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.011
MyCampusView Academy.

2
00:00:03.061 --> 00:00:06.643
AI for Young Builders, video 4.

3
00:00:06.693 --> 00:00:09.690
How computers see: pixels.

4
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.032
You see a smiley face.

5
00:00:13.082 --> 00:00:16.481
A computer sees a grid of numbers.

6
00:00:16.531 --> 00:00:18.510
Let us find out why.

7
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:24.478
Zoom in far enough on any digital picture and you find tiny

8
00:00:24.528 --> 00:00:26.223
squares called pixels.

9
00:00:26.273 --> 00:00:31.456
In a black-and-white picture each pixel can be stored as a number:

10
00:00:31.506 --> 00:00:33.835
one for black, zero for white.

11
00:00:33.885 --> 00:00:37.720
So this smiley face is really sixty-four numbers.

12
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:44.415
Grey pictures use a number from zero to two hundred and fifty-five

13
00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:45.645
for each pixel.

14
00:00:45.695 --> 00:00:51.224
Colour pictures use three numbers per pixel: how much red, green and

15
00:00:51.274 --> 00:00:52.701
blue light to mix.

16
00:00:52.751 --> 00:00:54.260
That is called RGB.

17
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.653
The numbers add up fast.

18
00:00:57.703 --> 00:01:00.475
Our smiley is sixty-four numbers.

19
00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:08.173
A small handwritten digit, twenty-eight by twenty-eight, is seven hundred and eighty-four.

20
00:01:08.223 --> 00:01:13.646
A tiny colour photo, a hundred by a hundred, is thirty thousand.

21
00:01:13.696 --> 00:01:15.870
A phone photo is millions.

22
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.141
Why does this matter?

23
00:01:19.191 --> 00:01:23.024
Computers can only calculate with numbers.

24
00:01:23.074 --> 00:01:25.151
An AI never sees a cat.

25
00:01:25.201 --> 00:01:31.714
It sees numbers, and learns which patterns of numbers usually mean cat.

26
00:01:31.764 --> 00:01:35.227
Similar pictures have similar numbers.

27
00:01:35.277 --> 00:01:39.480
Remember that, because your model will use it.

28
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.253
Let us recap.

29
00:01:42.303 --> 00:01:45.258
A picture is a grid of pixels.

30
00:01:45.308 --> 00:01:48.565
Each pixel is stored as a number.

31
00:01:48.615 --> 00:01:53.274
Colour pixels have red, green and blue numbers.

32
00:01:53.324 --> 00:01:56.480
Next: Train your first AI model.
